================================================================================
ATTRIBUTION
================================================================================

The collision model implemented in loracap/core.py (channel and
spreading-factor orthogonality, the capture effect, and the preamble timing
criterion) is a Python 3 re-implementation of the model introduced in
LoRaSim 0.2.1:

    Martin Bor, Utz Roedig, Thiemo Voigt and Juan Alonso,
    "Do LoRa Low-Power Wide-Area Networks Scale?",
    MSWiM '16, https://doi.org/10.1145/2988287.2989163
    Source: https://github.com/mcbor/lorasim

    LoRaSim 0.2.1 is Copyright (c) 2016 Thiemo Voigt <thiemo@sics.se> and
    Martin Bor <m.bor@lancaster.ac.uk>, and is licensed under the Creative
    Commons Attribution 4.0 International License (CC BY 4.0),
    https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

CC BY 4.0 permits derivative works under different terms provided attribution
is given, which this notice supplies. No LoRaSim source code is copied into
this package. The original is Python 2; the model here was reimplemented and
extended with direct multi-channel simulation, an enforced
simultaneous-demodulation limit, and polygon-based node placement. The
differences are documented in README.md and in the docstring of core.py.

Receiver sensitivity values in loracap/presets.py are reproduced from
manufacturer datasheets as factual reference data. Each carries its source in
that file.

================================================================================
DATA, OUTPUTS AND FIGURES
================================================================================

The MIT Licence above covers the software. It does not cover the data shipped
with it: site boundaries (studies/*/site_polygon.py), and simulation outputs
and figures (studies/*/outputs/). Those are licensed under the Creative Commons
Attribution 4.0 International License (CC BY 4.0),
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

Creative Commons licences are not recommended for software, and MIT does not
fit data well, which is why the two are separated rather than one licence being
stretched over both.
